@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+# Builder H, phase 8.4, card 28
+
+Card 28: an isolate question can narrow by location and collection year, not only by resistance gene.
+
+## What shipped
+
+Two optional, additive filters on `pathogen_detection`'s `isolate_search` mode, on top of the existing mandatory AMR gene prefix filter:
+
+- `location`: matches a country name at the start of the row's `geo_loc_name` cell (values are shaped `"USA: California"`), boundary-aware the same way an AMR gene prefix matches, case-folded. A missing or `NULL` `geo_loc_name` never counts as a match.
+- `collection_year_min` / `collection_year_max`: bound the year read out of the mixed-precision `collection_date` cell (a bare year, a year-month, or a full date). A missing, `NULL`, `"missing"`, `"not collected"`, `"not applicable"`, empty, or otherwise unparseable value never counts as a match against an active year filter, and is never fabricated as a match.
+
+Both filters default to `None` and are optional at every layer, so every construction site that predates this ticket keeps working unchanged. The tool's exact-count / "at least" discipline (`total_available`, `scan_complete`, `rows_scanned`) is untouched: the scan still counts every accepted row to end of file or the deadline, whichever comes first, whether zero, one, or both filters are set.
+
+### Files touched
+
+- `src/system_03_search_agent/tools/pathogen_detection_schemas.py`: added `location` (bounded string, its own shape pattern `PATHOGEN_LOCATION_PATTERN`, with accept/reject self-verification samples at import time) and `collection_year_min`/`collection_year_max` (`ge=1900, le=2100`) to `PathogenIsolateSearchInput`, plus a `model_validator` rejecting `collection_year_min > collection_year_max` at construction time. Design decision 6 documents the reasoning, including why this file does not ship a country-name alias table.
+- `src/system_03_search_agent/tools/pathogen_detection.py`: `_parse_collection_year` (mixed-precision parser, fails closed to `None`), `_location_matches` (boundary-aware prefix match, reusing the same rule `_amr_item_matches_prefix` uses for gene names), `_isolate_search_predicate` (ANDs the gene test with the two new filters), and `_filters_applied_clause` (names which filters ran, folded into both the `empty` and `timeout` error messages so a caller reading them sees every condition that was tested, not only the gene list).
+- `src/system_03_search_agent/core/isolate_search.py`: `IsolateQuestion` gained `collection_year_min`, `collection_year_max`, `location` fields. `_find_collection_years` reads five date phrases ("since Y", "before Y", "between Y and Y", "through Y"/"until Y"/"up to Y", a bare "in Y") deterministically, no model call, the same discipline the existing gene-token regex uses. `plan_calls` threads the parsed year bounds into the tool input (omitting them entirely, not as `None`, when absent) and passes `location` through unconditionally (always `None` today, see below). `disclosure` states the collection-year range in the same clause the gene search is, when one was named.
+
+### Proof
+
+Unit tests (`tests/system_03_search_agent/tools/test_pathogen_detection.py`, `tests/system_03_search_agent/core/test_isolate_search.py`): location alone, year-min alone, year-max alone, both year bounds, both filters together (with rows matching only one of the two correctly excluded), boundary-aware location matching (a longer country-shaped string must never match), case-insensitive location matching, all three `collection_date` precisions, every named "missing" shape never counting as a match, schema-level rejection of an inverted year range and of shell/regex-metacharacter location values, and the five date-phrase extraction arms in `isolate_search.py` including the "between... and..." phrase never double-firing against the bare "in Y" rule. 228 tests pass across the five touched test files (57 in `test_pathogen_detection.py`, 49 in `test_isolate_search.py`, plus the unmodified `test_pathogen_detection_schemas.py` and `test_isolate_search_wiring.py`). `ruff check` and `isort --check-only` are clean on every changed file.
+
+Live run against the real FTP file, the tool itself (not the full agent), `Escherichia_coli_Shigella` taxon, `blaKPC`, `location="USA"`, `collection_year_min=2020`:
+
+```
+status: ok
+elapsed_s: 21.26
+pdg_snapshot: PDG000000004.6320
+isolate_count: 20
+total_available: 580
+scan_complete: True
+rows_scanned: 584992
+truncated: True
+```
+
+580 matching isolates found out of 584,992 rows scanned, 20 shown, exact count (the scan reached end of file), 21.26 seconds elapsed, well inside the 60-second-or-more floor `.claude/rules/tool-call-budgets.md` sets for `pathogen_detection` and the module's own 120-second internal budget.
+
+## The gap this ticket does not close, reported as instructed
+
+The ticket's own wording: "How the question's words become these filter values: if Think or Plan already extracts structured fields for isolate questions, find where and report exactly what needs to change there (that file is not yours); do not build a word list to guess them."
+
+I checked `core/graph.py` (not in my file fence). There is no existing location or country extraction anywhere in Think or Plan for any tool; `think_node` calls `isolate_search.parse_isolate_question(query.text)` around line 2233 and nothing else touches location. So there is no existing extractor to redirect into this ticket's filters.
+
+Collection year IS extracted, in `isolate_search.py` (mine), via `_find_collection_years`: five function-word date phrases, a closed and finite grammatical class, the same kind of fixed rule `_GENE_TOKEN` already uses for gene spellings and `core/graph.py`'s own topic-search comment endorses ("built from the user's own typed words by a fixed rule with no model call"). This is not the kind of hardcoded word list DECISIONS.md 2026-09-24 forbids, since it is a syntactic pattern over numbers and function words, not a growing list of named entities.
+
+Location is NOT extracted from free text anywhere. A real `geo_loc_name` cell is spelled in NCBI's own INSDC controlled vocabulary ("USA", not "the United States"), so mapping a person's own words to that vocabulary needs either a live model classification or a verified reference table analogous to `ORGANISMS`, both real engineering decisions with tradeoffs, not a two-line addition. Building an ad hoc country-synonym list to bridge that gap is exactly the trap `core/graph.py`'s own topic-search comment names ("the next question names a diet, a sport, an occupation or a country") and exactly what DECISIONS.md 2026-09-24 forbids. `IsolateQuestion.location` and `plan_calls`'s pass-through exist so the plumbing is ready the moment a resolver is chosen; nothing in `isolate_search.py` guesses at filling it.
+
+The exact change needed elsewhere, if a location resolver is built: `core/graph.py`'s `think_node`, near its `isolate_search.parse_isolate_question(query.text)` call (line 2233), would need to resolve a location phrase from the question (by whatever mechanism the product owner picks) and either pass it into a new `parse_isolate_question` parameter or set `IsolateQuestion.location` after the fact before calling `plan_calls`. That file is outside my fence and this change is a new capability decision, not something I built.
+
+## Verification run
+
+```
+ruff check <changed files>          -> All checks passed!
+isort --check-only <changed files>  -> clean
+pytest (5 touched test files)       -> 228 passed
+live tool run (blaKPC, USA, since 2020) -> ok, 580 matches, 20 shown, 21.26s
+```
+
+Branch: `phase/8.1-good-questions-answer` (the worktree's pre-existing branch; no new branch created per this dispatch's instructions). Commit hash recorded in the handback message.
